```java
@Service
public class IdempotencyService {
    public <T> T execute(String requestId, String fingerprint, Class<T> type, int successStatus, Supplier<T> action)
```

Note: must be called within an existing transaction (business service @Transactional). It participates in that tx. On duplicate key insert failure (concurrent), catch DuplicateKeyException, re-select.

Wait — issue: if same-fingerprint replay returns stored response, we return deserialized stored body. For "同键同参返回首次结果" — return stored response with stored status. Controller needs the status too. Let me return a small wrapper: `IdemOutcome<T> { T body; int status; boolean replayed; }`. Controllers then `ResponseEntity.status(outcome.status()).body(outcome.body())`.

Simpler: services return DTO, controller always 200. But "同键同参返回首次结果" — if first result was 200 with body, replay returns same body 200. For error cases (e.g., assessment rejected is 200 business result; conflicts throw exceptions — those aren't stored). So storing only successful results with status 200 is fine. But renewal success maybe 200 too. Keep status column for completeness; always 200 in practice. Actually let's store 200 always. Simplify: execute returns T (deserialized on replay). Controller returns 200.

Hmm but should replay be distinguishable? Not required. Keep simple: return body.

Implementation:

```java
@Service
public class IdempotencyService {
    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;
    private final ObjectMapper mapper;

    public <T> T execute(String requestId, String fingerprint, Class<T> type, Supplier<T> action) {
        IdemRow existing = find(requestId);
        if (existing != null) return replay(existing, fingerprint, type);
        try {
            jdbc.update("INSERT INTO idempotency_record(request_id, fingerprint) VALUES (?,?)", requestId, fingerprint);
        } catch (DuplicateKeyException e) {
            IdemRow again = find(requestId);
            if (again == null) throw new ConflictException("幂等记录冲突，请重试");
            return replay(again, fingerprint, type);
        }
        T result = action.get();
        try {
            jdbc.update("UPDATE idempotency_record SET http_status=200, response_body=? WHERE request_id=?",
                mapper.writeValueAsString(result), requestId);
        } catch (JsonProcessingException e) { throw new IllegalStateException(e); }
        return result;
    }

    private <T> T replay(IdemRow row, String fingerprint, Class<T> type) {
        if (!row.fingerprint.equals(fingerprint)) throw new ConflictException("相同 requestId 但业务参数不一致");
        if (row.responseBody == null) throw new ConflictException("相同 requestId 的请求正在处理中，请稍后重试");
        try { return mapper.readValue(row.responseBody, type); }
        catch (JsonProcessingException e) { throw new IllegalStateException(e); }
    }
}
```

Concern: concurrent insert — tx B blocks on insert until tx A commits; then DuplicateKeyException; then `find` in tx B (READ_COMMITTED) sees A's committed row including response_body. Good.

Concern 2: H2 — after a failed INSERT due to unique violation, can the transaction continue? In H2, yes, statement failure doesn't abort transaction. OK.

Concern 3: For generic type T with nested generics (e.g., List<...>) — my responses are concrete DTO classes, fine.

CLOB read via JdbcTemplate: `queryForObject(..., String.class)` on CLOB works (H2 returns String). Use `(rs, i) -> rs.getString(...)`.

Also need DTOs. Let me now write IdempotencyService + DTO records in one step, then compile.

